g any new types. If you can paste the source text as well, I’ll perform the extraction directly.", "DELEUZE:Deleuze is a French philosopher whose translator's foreword describes his distinctive style: concise, pellucid sentences that ramify and link concepts; he promotes a philosophy of simultaneous mobility and closure of concepts and often uses c'est as a connector, engaging with Leibniz's ideas.", "LEIBNIZ:Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz (1646–1716) was a German philosopher, mathematician, and polymath whose work spans mathematics, logic, metaphysics, and the rational organization of knowledge. He co-developed differential calculus and viewed calculus as a powerful instrument for probing transcendental problems, while in some discussions his calculus is linked to psychic or perceptual mechanisms rather than a purely physical process. In geometry and analysis he proposedBaroque ideas about variation, suggesting families of curves parameterized by constants and the notion of seeking a tangent across an infinity of points, as well as considering inverses of tangents within a framework of continuous variation.\n\nLeibniz is best known for his Monadology, the theory that the universe is composed of indivisible, immaterial substances called monads. He argued that monads are windowless points of force that reflect the entire cosmos from their own perspective, and that minds (souls) are a form of monad whose perception unfolds within a universal harmony preestablished among all monads. This harmony, often described as preestablished or ideal causality, links separate substances without direct causal interaction, a view contrasted with Cartesian space and later contrasted in discussions of occasionalism and the mind–body problem. The soul’s activity is framed as a point of view within a larger unity, with notions of interiority, succession, and the folding and unfolding of matter, and with intrinsic causal forces shaping perception and action.\n\nLeibniz also developed influential philosophical concepts such as the principle of sufficient reason, the law of continuity, and the distinction between predicates as relations, as well as theories about space, time, and predication. He introduced ideas about infinitesimal relations, inclusion, and the unity of perception, and he described extension as the continuous repetition of position—an idea that ties space to the multiplicity of point-of-view relations. In various passages he is cited as discussing the two floors of being—the upper, spiritual or mental, and the lower, material—and how their inseparability can produce a seamless continuity between matter and mind. He is invoked in thought experiments such as the “chess-playing God” to explore chance, causality, and moral models, and his influence extends to later thinkers such as Kant, Whitehead, and Bergson, who grapple with monadic relations, harmony, and the emergence of novelty.\n\nLeibniz’s work thus fuses mathematical innovation with a rigorous metaphysical program, proposing that the world is a harmony of monads whose differential relations generate perception, meaning, and the structure of reality.", "LOCKE:Locke is referenced as a philosopher whose ideas Leibniz seeks to conciliate within the discussion of the soul and matter.", "DESANES:Desanes is mentioned as someone who cannot navigate the labyrinth, illustrating the search for continuity in rectilinear tracks.", "HUYGENS:Huygens develops Baroque mathematical physics focused on curvilinearity, aligning with Leibniz on the curvature and dynamics of matter and the universe.", "WOLMIN:Wolmin notes Baroque traits in matter, describing features like the lower floor widening, pediment shaping, stairs, and vortex-like motions.", "DESCARTES:Ren é Descartes is a 17th-century French philosopher and mathematician who stands as a foundational figure in modern Western philosophy. He is best known for formulating methodological doubt and for the cogito ergo sum—“I think, therefore I am”—which anchors his rationalist program, epistemology, and the mind–body problem. Descartes presents the Cogito as a core, indubitable bedrock of knowledge and, in some readings, as a way to define humanity beyond traditional genus-based definitions (such as “rational animal”). He argues that thinking is the essence of the self, yielding the universal form of psychic life expressed in the formula “I think myself,” with the self described as the properly psychic organism whose faculties illuminate the I. This framework underpins his critique of definitional approaches that rely on genus and difference, and it situates the mind as central to the identification and individuation of the subject.\n\nDescartes is also celebrated for Cartesian coordinates, the mathematical achievement that unites algebra and geometry and which reflects his broader methodological ambition: to name concepts precisely and to clarify how language designates conceptual entities. In philosophy and science, his use of proper names to designate abstract concepts is discussed as part of how names link to their referents, reinforcing a systematic method of thinking about ideas.\n\nHis mind–body philosophy—often presented as a dualism between mind (res cogitans) and body (res extensa)—is a recurring touchstone in the history of science and philosophy. Spinoza engages with Descartes’ ideas on the mind–body relation, and Descartes’ suggestions about the inverse relation in Traité des passions are cited in discussions about how mental and bodily states interact. Leibniz, in turn, critiques the Cartesian view, imputing to Descartes a belief in a perfectly fluid matter and the error of separability. These exchanges position Descartes as a central interlocutor in the dialogue on the nature of substance, causality, and the unity of experience.\n\nMoreover, Descartes’ influence extends to discussions of the self and the act of thinking as the basis for knowledge and identity, and his writings—such as the Replies to Arnaud—are cited as sources of suggestion and influence in the unfolding debate over rationalism, individuation, and the essence of thought. Overall, Descartes emerges as a pivotal figure whose methodological doubt, Cogito, mind–body distinction, and mathematical and linguistic innovations underpin a lasting legacy in philosophy and the sciences.", "VAN HELMONT:Johann Baptista van Helmont, a 17th-century Flemish chemist and alchemist cited in the text as an influence on Leibniz's thinking about matter and vital forces.", "BOYLE:Robert Boyle, a 17th-century Irish natural philosopher and chemist whose experiments inspire the discussion of matter, plastic forces, and transformation in the text.", "BOYLE'S EXPERIMENT:Boyle's experiments mentioned in the text that influence the discussion of matter, force, and the transformation of raw matter into organic matter.) <|COMPLETE|>", "HARLEQUIN:A fictional character from the commedia dell'arte, used in the text as a metaphorical reference for clothing layers and the distinction between inner and outer states.", "MANDELBROT:Benoit Mandelbrot is the mathematician associated with fractal geometry; in the text, Mandelbrot's fractal dimension is referenced as a fractional or irrational, non-dimensional measure that arises from the relationship between curved and straight lines and the concept of variation in fractal structures.)<|COMPLETE|>", "BERNARD CACHE:Bernard Cache is a philosopher, theorist, and architect whose work interweaves philosophy of technology with architectural theory. He demonstrates a modern conception of the technological object, illustrating a view in which objects are defined by fluctuation and temporal modulation rather than by a fixed mold. In his architectural theory and practice, Cache is described as making architecture the first art of the frame, and he catalogs enframing forms that structure the edifice without prescribing fixed content. Taken together, Cache’s ideas link the dynamic, process-oriented nature of technology with an architectural approach that emphasizes framing and openness, where structure and form guide interpretation without cementing a single, determinate content.", "NIETZSCHE:NIETZSCHE is a 19th-century German philosopher and cultural critic whose work has profoundly influenced modern thought. The text references him as strictly Spinozist in relation to Spinoza’s ideas, and it discusses Zarathustra and his metamorphosis as associated with Nietzsche’s role in articulating related philosophical concepts, including his authorship of Thus Spoke Zarathustra. He is cited for claims that culture is an involuntary adventure and for discussions on training the mind and producing a “nation of thinkers.” The discussion foregrounds his concept of the will to power, wherein powerlessness is transmuted into agency and used to frame understandings of chance, problem-solving, and the dynamics of power in society. Nietzsche is described as a German philosopher whose writings critique morality, religion, and the foundations of society; within the text, he is invoked in relation to the idea of the “evaluating eye,” punishment, and the breaking of a phonographic connection, signaling a tension between perception, judgment, and signification. The text also engages with his ideas on repetition and the eternal return, noting Nietzsche’s association with the concept while sometimes indicating he gave no explicit exposition of it. Persistently, Nietzsche is situated within perspectivism—the view that multiple, divergent viewpoints exist and are linked only by distance and resonance—depicted as a deeper art than Leibniz’s point of view, and described as opening onto a divergence that corresponds to different “towns” or perspectives. He is connected to a continuum from Lucretius in pursuing a naturalist end and to the notion that selection is often exerted in favor of large numbers, used to explain how social aggregates arise under selective pressure and to frame modern thought on culture and society. The text also notes his role in the critique of life through the phrase Avant Nietzsche and discusses his influence on the correction of ideas, as well as his emphasis that philosophy must create concepts rather than passively receive them, present them, and make them convincing. Additionally, it mentions his engagement with the relationship between the creation of concepts and taste, describing taste as a faculty enabling concept creation. Overall, NIETZSCHE is portrayed as a central figure whose exploration of power, knowledge, culture, and the generation of concepts has shaped discussions of morality, religion, and the nature of philosophical inquiry.", "WILLIAM JAMES:William James is an American philosopher and psychologist mentioned in the text. In conjunction with Russen, he uses the monads-vs-bodies distinction to advance the philosophical argument. He is also referenced as one of the James brothers involved in perspectivism."], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-large"}}, "key": "embeddings_72f412cb023f8ae487487e3a547840765b28a30aa8d7d0dd52c46daa2deaf705_v2"}